"""Scene definitions for the Cross-Site Scripting: Output Encoding and CSP teaching video (generated from concept.toml)."""

SCENES = [
    dict(key='01-poster', kind='poster', title='Cross-Site Scripting: Output Encoding and CSP',
         body=None,
         narration="Hello, and welcome. [[slnc 400]] This video explains cross site scripting, X S S for short, and the defences that work, in Java. [[slnc 300]] This video is presented by Jayasekhar Konduru. [[slnc 600]] First, a simple definition. [[slnc 300]] Cross site scripting happens when text from a user is read by the browser as part of the page. [[slnc 600]] Think of a shop's notice board. [[slnc 300]] Customers pin up notes, and the shop pins up signs. [[slnc 300]] If a customer's note looks exactly like a shop sign, people follow it. [[slnc 300]] Encoding puts every customer note behind glass, with a label. [[slnc 700]] In this video, the domain is an online store, and its product reviews. [[slnc 500]] By the end, you will hear how a review can change a page. [[slnc 300]] How to encode text for each place it goes. [[slnc 300]] Why filtering input is not the fix. [[slnc 300]] And how a strict content security policy adds a second line of defence."),
    dict(key='02-scenario', kind='bullets', title='The Scenario',
         body=['An online store shows product reviews.', '', 'The author, a fit note and the text', 'all come from customers.', '', "Every visitor's browser reads the page."],
         narration="Here is the scenario. [[slnc 400]] An online store shows product reviews. [[slnc 500]] The author's name, a short note about fit, and the text, all come from customers. [[slnc 500]] And every visitor's browser reads the page, including visitors who are signed in."),
    dict(key='03-words', kind='bullets', title='The Words',
         body=['Cross-site scripting: user text read as page code', 'Output encoding: text made safe for one place', 'Context: between tags, attribute, URL, script', 'Content-Security-Policy: which scripts may run', 'Nonce: a random value, new for every page'],
         narration='Before the demos, the words. [[slnc 500]] Cross site scripting means text from a user is read as part of the page. [[slnc 500]] Output encoding makes text safe for the one place it is written. [[slnc 500]] The context is that place: between tags, in an attribute, in a link, or in a script. [[slnc 500]] A content security policy tells the browser which scripts may run. [[slnc 500]] And a nonce is a random value, new for every page.'),
    dict(key='04-letters-1', kind='bullets', title='What the Letters Stand For',
         body=['XSS: Cross-Site Scripting: text from a user read as part of the page', 'HTML: HyperText Markup Language: the markup of a web page', 'CSP: Content-Security-Policy: a header listing which scripts may run', 'URL: Uniform Resource Locator: the address of a page', 'HTTP, GET: HyperText Transfer Protocol; GET asks the server for a page', 'HttpOnly: a cookie flag: scripts on the page cannot read the cookie'],
         narration='A few letters come up again and again, so here is what they stand for. [[slnc 400]] X S S is cross site scripting: text from a user that the browser reads as part of the page. [[slnc 400]] H T M L is hypertext markup language: the tags that make up a web page. [[slnc 400]] C S P is content security policy: a response header that tells the browser which scripts may run. [[slnc 400]] U R L is uniform resource locator: the address of a page, with its parameters. [[slnc 400]] H T T P is hypertext transfer protocol. A get request asks the server for a page. [[slnc 400]] H T T P only is a cookie flag. Scripts on the page cannot read a cookie that carries it.'),
    dict(key='05-letters-2', kind='bullets', title='What the Letters Stand For (continued)',
         body=['Base64, hex: Base64: bytes as letters, digits, + and /; hex: base sixteen, 0-9 and a-f', 'JDK: Java Development Kit', 'CWE: Common Weakness Enumeration: CWE-79 is cross-site scripting'],
         narration='Base sixty four writes bytes with letters, digits, plus and slash. Hex is base sixteen, with the digits zero to nine and a to f. [[slnc 400]] J D K is the Java development kit. [[slnc 400]] C W E is the common weakness enumeration. Number seventy nine is cross site scripting.'),
    dict(key='06-text-or-markup', kind='figure', title='The same text, pasted or encoded',
         body='text_or_markup',
         narration="Let's see the whole idea on one picture. [[slnc 400]] At the top, Ana's review, exactly as she typed it. [[slnc 700]] On the left, the shop pastes it into the page as it is. [[slnc 300]] The browser reads the angle brackets as a tag. [[slnc 300]] It finds a bold element, and shows great boots in bold. [[slnc 300]] The review has become part of the page. [[slnc 700]] On the right, the shop encodes it first. [[slnc 300]] Each angle bracket becomes a short code, ampersand l t, or ampersand g t. [[slnc 300]] The browser shows those codes as the characters themselves. [[slnc 300]] Ana sees exactly what she typed, and the page has no new element. [[slnc 700]] Same text. [[slnc 300]] Pasted, it is markup. [[slnc 300]] Encoded, it stays text."),
    dict(key='07-act-1-figure', kind='figure', title='What the browser finds when a review is pasted in',
         body='review_pasted',
         narration="First demo: a review that changes the page. [[slnc 400]] On the left, the review box. [[slnc 300]] Ana types her review, with a bold tag around the words: great boots. [[slnc 700]] In the middle, the page as the shop builds it. [[slnc 300]] White is the shop's own markup. [[slnc 300]] Red is what Ana typed, pasted in as it is. [[slnc 700]] On the right, the elements a browser finds. [[slnc 300]] With plain text, the shop's six elements. [[slnc 300]] With Ana's review, one more: a bold element the shop never wrote. [[slnc 700]] At the bottom, Eve does the same with a script tag. [[slnc 300]] Now the extra element is a script. [[slnc 300]] The browser cannot tell it from the shop's own code, so it would run."),
    dict(key='08-act-1-1', kind='console', title='Act One — A review that changes the page',
         body='ONE. A review that changes the page.\n  THE REVIEW (typed by Ana):\n    <b>Great boots</b>\n  THE PAGE AS BUILT (the review pasted in as it is):\n    <div class="review" data-fit="true to size">\n      <p><b>Great boots</b></p>\n      <span>Ana</span></div>\n  THE RESULT (the elements a browser finds):\n    plain text    html body h1 div p span\n    Ana\'s review  html body h1 div p b span\n    1 b element the shop never wrote  <- THE PAGE CHANGED',
         narration="Here is the real output. [[slnc 400]] The input: Ana's review. [[slnc 400]] The page as built, with the review pasted in. [[slnc 400]] And the result: the elements a browser finds. [[slnc 300]] Plain text gives six. [[slnc 300]] Ana's review gives seven: one bold element the shop never wrote."),
    dict(key='09-act-1-2', kind='console', title='Act One — A review that changes the page',
         body="ONE. (continued)\n  THE REVIEW (typed by Eve):\n    <script>steal(document.cookie)</script>\n  THE RESULT:\n    script elements: 1  <- IT RUNS AS THE SHOP'S OWN CODE\n    it could act as the signed-in customer",
         narration="Eve's review is a script tag. [[slnc 400]] The result: one script element on the page. [[slnc 300]] It would run as the shop's own code, and could act as the signed in customer."),
    dict(key='10-act-2-figure', kind='figure', title='Four places, four encoders',
         body='encoders',
         narration='Second demo: encoding for the place the text goes. [[slnc 400]] Each row is one place where the page writes customer text. [[slnc 300]] On the left, the text before encoding. [[slnc 300]] On the right, after. [[slnc 300]] The characters that changed are in green. [[slnc 700]] Between tags, the angle brackets become ampersand l t, and ampersand g t. [[slnc 300]] The browser shows them, but never reads them as a tag. [[slnc 600]] In an attribute, the quote mark becomes ampersand quot, so it cannot end the attribute. [[slnc 600]] In a link, a space becomes percent twenty, and an ampersand becomes percent twenty six. [[slnc 600]] In a script string, a quote becomes backslash u, and four hex digits. [[slnc 700]] Four places. [[slnc 300]] Four different encoders. [[slnc 300]] Using the wrong one, or none, is enough to break the page.'),
    dict(key='11-act-2-1', kind='console', title='Act Two — Encoding for the place the text goes',
         body='TWO. Encoding for the place the text goes.\n  BEFORE ENCODING (the place, and the customer\'s text):\n    between tags      <b>Great boots</b>\n    quoted attribute  Fits 5" boots\n    URL parameter     size 5 & up\n    script string     It\'s "great"\n  AFTER ENCODING:\n    between tags      &lt;b&gt;Great boots&lt;/b&gt;\n    quoted attribute  Fits 5&quot; boots\n    URL parameter     size%205%20%26%20up\n    script string     It\\u0027s \\u0022great\\u0022',
         narration="Here is the output. [[slnc 400]] Before encoding: the place, and the customer's text. [[slnc 400]] After encoding: the same four values, each made safe for its place."),
    dict(key='12-act-2-2', kind='console', title='Act Two — Encoding for the place the text goes',
         body='TWO. (continued)\n  HOW EACH CHARACTER IS ENCODED:\n    char   HTML    URL  script\n    <      &lt;    %3C  \\u003c\n    >      &gt;    %3E  \\u003e\n    &      &amp;   %26  \\u0026\n    "      &quot;  %22  \\u0022\n    \'      &#39;   %27  \\u0027\n    space  space   %20  space\n    " is character 34 = 2 x 16 + 2, so 22 in hex',
         narration="How each character is encoded, in each place. [[slnc 400]] The U R L and script codes are the character's number in hexadecimal. [[slnc 300]] A double quote is character thirty four. [[slnc 300]] Thirty four is two sixteens and two, so twenty two in hex."),
    dict(key='13-act-2-3', kind='console', title='Act Two — Encoding for the place the text goes',
         body='TWO. (continued)\n  THE ENCODED PAGE (Ben\'s review):\n    <div class="review" data-fit="Fits 5&quot; boots">\n      <p>Warm &amp; dry, price &lt; 20 &amp; &gt; 10</p>\n      <span>Ben</span>\n      <a href="/reviews?author=Ben">more</a></div>\n  THE RESULT (what the browser reads back):\n    fit note     Fits 5" boots\n    review text  Warm & dry, price < 20 & > 10\n    Ana\'s review, encoded: b elements 0',
         narration="Ben's review, on the encoded page. [[slnc 400]] The quote in his fit note is ampersand quot, and the brackets in his text are entities. [[slnc 400]] The result: the browser reads back exactly what Ben typed. [[slnc 300]] And Ana's review, encoded, has no bold element."),
    dict(key='14-act-3-figure', kind='figure', title='A filter deletes too much, and misses too much',
         body='filter_misses',
         narration="Third demo: why filtering input is not the fix. [[slnc 400]] The filter deletes every angle bracket. [[slnc 600]] In the top row, Ben's honest review. [[slnc 300]] Price less than twenty, and more than ten. [[slnc 300]] The filter deletes the two brackets, shown in red, and the sentence no longer makes sense. [[slnc 700]] In the middle row, Eve's fit note. [[slnc 300]] It has no angle brackets at all. [[slnc 300]] So the filter lets it through, unchanged. [[slnc 700]] At the bottom, what the browser finds on the review's div. [[slnc 300]] Eve's quote closed the attribute early. [[slnc 300]] Now there is an on click attribute, with her script in it. [[slnc 700]] Encoded for the attribute instead, her quotes become text, and the div keeps only its two attributes."),
    dict(key='15-act-3-1', kind='console', title='Act Three — Why filtering input is not the fix',
         body='THREE. Why filtering input is not the fix.\n  BEFORE THE FILTER (Ben\'s review, Eve\'s fit note):\n    Warm & dry, price < 20 & > 10\n    x" onclick="steal()\n  AFTER A FILTER THAT DELETES < AND >:\n    Warm & dry, price  20 &  10  <- HONEST WORDS CHANGED\n    x" onclick="steal()  <- THE ATTACK IS UNTOUCHED\n  THE PAGE AS BUILT (the filtered fit note pasted in):\n    <div class="review" data-fit="x" onclick="steal()">\n  THE RESULT (the attributes a browser finds on the div):\n    class=review  data-fit=x  onclick=steal()\n    <- A SCRIPT RUNS WHEN THE REVIEW IS CLICKED',
         narration="Here is the output. [[slnc 400]] Before the filter: Ben's review, and Eve's fit note. [[slnc 400]] After the filter: Ben's words are changed, and Eve's attack is untouched. [[slnc 400]] The page as built, and the result: the div now has an on click attribute."),
    dict(key='16-act-3-2', kind='console', title='Act Three — Why filtering input is not the fix',
         body='THREE. (continued)\n  ENCODED INSTEAD (the div\'s data-fit attribute):\n    data-fit="x&quot; onclick=&quot;steal()"\n  THE RESULT:\n    class=review  data-fit=x" onclick="steal()\n    Ben\'s words read back exactly: true\n  a filter guesses; an encoder is chosen for the place',
         narration="Encoded for the attribute instead. [[slnc 400]] The quotes become ampersand quot. [[slnc 300]] The result: only class and data fit, holding Eve's exact text. [[slnc 300]] And Ben's words read back exactly."),
    dict(key='17-act-4-figure', kind='figure', title="Only the script that carries this page's nonce runs",
         body='csp_nonce',
         narration="Fourth demo: a strict content security policy, with a fresh nonce. [[slnc 400]] On the left, how the nonce is made. [[slnc 300]] Sixteen random bytes. [[slnc 300]] Base sixty four turns every three bytes into four characters. [[slnc 300]] Six groups, so twenty four characters. [[slnc 700]] At the top right, the response header. [[slnc 300]] Its script source names that nonce. [[slnc 600]] Below it, two scripts on the page. [[slnc 300]] The shop's own script carries the same nonce, so the browser runs it. [[slnc 300]] Eve's script got onto the page without a nonce. [[slnc 300]] It cannot know the value, because it is new for every page. [[slnc 300]] So the browser blocks it. [[slnc 700]] Encoding is the fix. [[slnc 300]] The policy is the second line of defence, for the one place encoding was forgotten."),
    dict(key='18-act-4-1', kind='console', title='Act Four — A strict Content-Security-Policy, with a fresh nonce',
         body='FOUR. A strict Content-Security-Policy, with a fresh nonce.\n  THE REQUEST (to the shop, served for real on this machine):\n    GET /product\n  THE RESPONSE (its nonce is shown as <nonce>):\n    status 200, and the header Content-Security-Policy:\n      default-src \'self\'\n      script-src \'nonce-<nonce>\' \'strict-dynamic\'\n      object-src \'none\'\n      base-uri \'none\'\n      frame-ancestors \'none\'\n    Set-Cookie: session=demo-24; Path=/;\n      HttpOnly; Secure; SameSite=Lax\n    <script nonce="<nonce>">var firstReviewer = "Ana";</script>',
         narration="Here is the output. [[slnc 400]] The request: get the product page, served for real. [[slnc 400]] The response: status two hundred, the policy, one directive per line, with the nonce shown as a placeholder. [[slnc 300]] An H T T P only cookie, and the shop's script with the same nonce."),
    dict(key='19-act-4-2', kind='console', title='Act Four — A strict Content-Security-Policy, with a fresh nonce',
         body="FOUR. (continued)\n  HOW THE NONCE IS MADE:\n    16 random bytes = 128 bits, written in Base64\n    Base64 turns every 3 bytes into 4 characters\n    16 bytes -> 6 groups (the last one short) -> 24 characters\n    example, bytes 00 01 02 ... 0f -> AAECAwQFBgcICQoLDA0ODw==\n    this page's nonce: 24 characters; the next page's differs: true\n    the script's nonce is the policy's nonce: true\n  WHICH SCRIPTS MAY RUN (what a browser decides):\n    the shop's script, with the nonce     yes\n    Eve's script, without a nonce         no  <- BLOCKED\n    the same script, with no policy       yes\n    'unsafe-inline' and no nonce          yes  <- PROTECTS NOTHING\n    the cookie is HttpOnly, so no script can read it: true",
         narration="How the nonce is made: sixteen random bytes, in base sixty four, so twenty four characters, and different on the next page. [[slnc 400]] And which scripts may run. [[slnc 300]] The shop's script, yes. [[slnc 300]] Eve's, no. [[slnc 300]] With no policy, or with unsafe inline, it would run."),
    dict(key='20-costs', kind='bullets', title='What It Costs',
         body=['Every place needs its own encoder:', '  one forgotten place is enough', 'Every inline script must carry the nonce', 'The nonce must be new for every response', 'In practice: a template that encodes by default'],
         narration="Nothing is free. [[slnc 400]] Here is what the defences cost. [[slnc 500]] This one page writes customer text into four kinds of place, and each needs its own encoder. [[slnc 300]] One forgotten place is enough. [[slnc 500]] The policy needs care. [[slnc 300]] Every inline script must carry the nonce, and every new script source is added on purpose. [[slnc 500]] The nonce must be new for every response, or a page from yesterday hands an attacker today's key. [[slnc 500]] In practice, a template engine that encodes by default does the first part, as the Spring Boot version of this project shows."),
    dict(key='21-ladder', kind='bullets', title='Fixes, Weakest First',
         body=['Filtering input: changes text, misses places', 'Encoding by hand: works, easy to forget', 'Templates that encode by default', 'Plus a strict, nonce-based policy', 'Plus HttpOnly session cookies'],
         narration="Let's put the fixes side by side, weakest first. [[slnc 400]] Filtering input changes honest text, and misses places. [[slnc 500]] Encoding by hand works, but one forgotten place is enough. [[slnc 500]] Templates that encode by default make the safe way the easy way. [[slnc 500]] A strict policy with a nonce stops what slips through. [[slnc 500]] And H T T P only cookies keep the session out of any script's reach."),
    dict(key='22-recognise', kind='bullets', title='Where You Have Seen It',
         body=['&lt; and &amp; in page source', 'CSP headers in developer tools', 'HttpOnly on session cookies', 'Advisories that say CWE-79'],
         narration="You have probably met this already. [[slnc 400]] The codes for less than and ampersand in a page's source are encoding at work. [[slnc 500]] Content security policy headers appear in the browser's developer tools. [[slnc 500]] Session cookies are often marked H T T P only. [[slnc 500]] And security advisories that mention C W E seventy nine are about this problem."),
    dict(key='23-verdict', kind='bullets', title='How To Use It',
         body=['Encode on output, for the context', 'Prefer templates that encode by default', 'Strict CSP with a fresh nonce', 'HttpOnly session cookies', 'Never rely on input filtering'],
         narration='So, how do you use it? [[slnc 400]] Encode on output, for the context, every time. [[slnc 500]] Prefer templates that encode by default. [[slnc 500]] Add a strict policy, with a fresh nonce for every response. [[slnc 500]] Mark session cookies H T T P only. [[slnc 500]] And never rely on filtering input as the fix.'),
    dict(key='24-classes', kind='bullets', title='The Java Classes',
         body=['ReviewPage: the product page, pasted or encoded', 'Encode: one encoder for each place text is written', 'NaiveFilter: the tempting fix that is not one', 'ContentSecurityPolicy: the nonce, the policy, and what may run', 'ShopServer: the real page, served on this machine'],
         narration='Now, a quick look at the code. [[slnc 300]] Everything runs in plain Java: a small model reads pages the way a browser does, and one real page is served on this machine. [[slnc 300]] Here are the classes that matter.'),
    dict(key='25-class-1', kind='code', title='ReviewPage — pasted as it is',
         body='// ReviewPage: the product page, pasted or encoded\nhtml.append("<div class=\\"review\\" data-fit=\\"")\n    .append(r.fitNote())\n    .append("\\"><p>")\n    .append(r.text())\n    .append("</p><span>")\n    .append(r.author())\n    .append("</span></div>");',
         narration="The unsafe page appends the customer's text straight into the markup. [[slnc 300]] A quote, or an angle bracket, in that text becomes part of the page."),
    dict(key='26-class-2', kind='code', title='ReviewPage — encoded for each place',
         body='// ReviewPage: the product page, pasted or encoded\nhtml.append("<div class=\\"review\\" data-fit=\\"")\n    .append(Encode.attribute(r.fitNote()))\n    .append("\\"><p>")\n    .append(Encode.htmlBody(r.text()))\n    .append("</p><span>")\n    .append(Encode.htmlBody(r.author()))\n    .append("</span>")\n    .append("<a href=\\"/reviews?author=")\n    .append(Encode.urlParameter(r.author()))',
         narration='The safe page writes the same values, but each one goes through the encoder for its place. [[slnc 300]] The attribute encoder, the body encoder, and the U R L encoder.'),
    dict(key='27-class-3', kind='code', title='Encode — between tags and in attributes',
         body='// Encode: one encoder for each place text is written\nswitch (c) {\n    case \'&\' -> out.append("&amp;");\n    case \'<\' -> out.append("&lt;");\n    case \'>\' -> out.append("&gt;");\n    case \'"\' -> out.append("&quot;");\n    case \'\\\'\' -> out.append("&#39;");\n    default -> out.append(c);',
         narration='Between tags and inside a quoted attribute, five characters are replaced by their H T M L entities. [[slnc 300]] The ampersand comes first in the list, so every code it writes is plain text.'),
    dict(key='28-class-4', kind='code', title='Encode — in a URL and in a script',
         body='// Encode: one encoder for each place text is written\nreturn URLEncoder.encode(text, StandardCharsets.UTF_8)\n    .replace("+", "%20");\n...\nout.append(String.format(java.util.Locale.ROOT,\n    "\\\\u%04x", (int) c));',
         narration='Inside a U R L, a parameter is percent encoded by the J D K. [[slnc 300]] In a script string, any character that is not a letter, digit or space becomes backslash u and four hex digits.'),
    dict(key='29-class-5', kind='code', title='NaiveFilter — delete two characters',
         body='// NaiveFilter: the tempting fix that is not one\nstatic String clean(String text) {\n    return text.replace("<", "").replace(">", "");',
         narration='The filter deletes angle brackets from every input. [[slnc 300]] It cannot know where the text will go, so it breaks honest text and misses the quote.'),
    dict(key='30-class-6', kind='code', title='ContentSecurityPolicy — nonce and policy',
         body='// ContentSecurityPolicy: the nonce, the policy, and what may run\nbyte[] bytes = new byte[NONCE_BYTES];\nRANDOM.nextBytes(bytes);\nreturn Base64.getEncoder().encodeToString(bytes);\n...\nreturn "default-src \'self\'; script-src \'nonce-"\n    + nonce + "\' \'strict-dynamic\'; object-src \'none\';\n...',
         narration='Sixteen bytes from a secure random source, in base sixty four, make the nonce. [[slnc 300]] The policy puts it in the script source list.'),
    dict(key='31-class-7', kind='code', title='ContentSecurityPolicy — which inline script may run',
         body='// ContentSecurityPolicy: the nonce, the policy, and what may run\nif (scriptNonce != null\n        && sources.contains("\'nonce-" + scriptNonce + "\'")) {\n    return true;\n}\nreturn sources.contains("\'unsafe-inline\'") && !hasNonceOrHash;',
         narration="A script runs if it carries this response's nonce. [[slnc 300]] Unsafe inline lets any script run, unless a nonce is listed, in which case browsers ignore it."),
    dict(key='32-class-8', kind='code', title='ShopServer — a new nonce for every response',
         body='// ShopServer: the real page, served on this machine\nString nonce = ContentSecurityPolicy.newNonce();\nbyte[] body = ReviewPage.safe(reviews, nonce)\n    .getBytes(StandardCharsets.UTF_8);\n...\n.add("Content-Security-Policy",\n     ContentSecurityPolicy.strict(nonce));\n...\n.add("Set-Cookie", "session=demo-" + nonce.length()\n     + "; Path=/; HttpOnly; Secure; SameSite=Lax");',
         narration="Every request gets a new nonce. [[slnc 300]] The same value goes into the page's script and into the policy header. [[slnc 300]] The session cookie is H T T P only."),
    dict(key='33-outro', kind='outro', title='Thanks for Watching',
         body=['Full source, notes, diagrams and an animated walkthrough', 'are in the repository. Add a fifth context', 'and decide how to encode it.'],
         narration="That's cross site scripting, and its defences. [[slnc 400]] If you remember one sentence, make it this one. [[slnc 300]] Text from a user must stay text, so encode it for the place it goes, and back that up with a strict policy. [[slnc 500]] The full source code, written notes, diagrams, and an animated walkthrough are all in the repository. [[slnc 300]] It runs on your own machine, with nothing installed except a Java development kit. [[slnc 500]] Here is one exercise to try. [[slnc 300]] Add a fifth context, a style value, and decide how to encode it. [[slnc 500]] If you found this useful, a like really helps other people discover it. [[slnc 300]] Kindly subscribe to the channel. [[slnc 300]] It helps the channel grow and bring you more content. [[slnc 400]] Thanks for watching!"),
]

VIDEO = {'footer': 'Cross-Site Scripting: Output Encoding and CSP  ·  Security Basics  ·  Java', 'poster': {'headline': [('CROSS-SITE SCRIPTING', 'text'), ('ENCODING + CSP', 'accent')], 'taglines': [('Text from a customer must stay text', 'gold')], 'before': {'code': 'html + review.text()', 'note': 'the review becomes the page', 'tag': 'BEFORE'}, 'after': {'code': 'Encode.htmlBody(text) + nonce CSP', 'note': 'text stays text', 'tag': 'AFTER'}}, 'highlight': {'console_green': ['AFTER ENCODING', 'ENCODED INSTEAD', 'THE ENCODED PAGE', 'HOW EACH CHARACTER', 'HOW THE NONCE'], 'console_rose': ['THE PAGE AS BUILT', 'AFTER A FILTER'], 'console_amber': ['THE REVIEW', 'THE RESULT', 'BEFORE ENCODING', 'BEFORE THE FILTER', 'WHICH SCRIPTS'], 'console_cyan': ['THE REQUEST', 'THE RESPONSE']}}
